 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Construction",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The coil is made of pure silver inner electrode layers stacked inside the ferrite body. Each end carries a silver termination plated with nickel and then tin."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Ratings across the range",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Q is measured at a 50 mV test signal, at 50 MHz for the lowest values falling to 1 MHz at 15 µH and above. Rated current steps down as inductance rises: 300 mA up to 0.082 µH, 250 mA from 0.10 to 0.33 µH, 200 mA at 0.39 and 0.47 µH, 150 mA from 0.56 to 0.82 µH, 100 mA at 1.0 and 1.2 µH, 50 mA from 1.5 to 3.9 µH, 25 mA from 4.7 to 10 µH, 15 mA at 12 µH and 5 mA from 15 µH up."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The parts are meant for reflow. During preheat the gap between part surface and solder temperature stays at 150℃ or less, and when the board is cooled into a solvent after soldering the gap stays at 100℃ or less; too little preheat can crack the ferrite. Time spent in repeated soldering passes adds up against the profile limits, and going beyond the profile can corrode the electrodes."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Storage",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Keep parts sealed, on shelving, away from heat shock, vibration and direct sunlight, and out of atmospheres holding sulfur, chlorine or acid, which oxidise the terminations and spoil solderability."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Materials",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "No ozone-depleting substances such as CCl4 or HCFC are used in manufacture. A part whose label reads unitary lead free meets RoHS."
  }
 ],
